The two checks disagreed: missed_words already accepted near spellings (Mana/Anna ratio 0.75), but word_error_rate counted the same word as a full substitution, and in a 4-word line one substitution exceeds MAX_WER. Making WER use the same _same_word rule keeps the two checks consistent instead of loosening MAX_WER, which would let a real wrong word through in every short line. The similarity rule applies only to words of CONTENT_WORD_LENGTH or more, because short words have high ratios by accident (a/an, is/it) and errors there change meaning. Accepting a near-miss name is a deliberate trade-off: ASR is least reliable on names, and the alternative was dropping the line entirely (the sample video lost its opening line). The rephrase prompt names the concrete failure ('not Mah nah for Mana') because the earlier 'written out as they are spoken' instruction was applied to the name itself.
